A dependency-free static blog: markdown files in `posts/`, plain HTML out in
`public/`. No framework, no database, nothing to install.

## Write and publish

```bash
npm run dev       # build + preview at http://localhost:3000
npm run build     # build only — writes public/
npm run drafts    # preview including draft: true posts
npm test          # test the generator
```

Add a post by dropping a markdown file in `posts/`:

```
---
title: What I shipped this week
date: 2026-08-01
summary: One sentence for the index page and the RSS feed.
tags: [shipping]
draft: false
---

Your post starts here.
```

- **Only `title` and `date` matter.** No `summary` means the first paragraph is used.
- **The filename is the URL.** `my-post.md` publishes to `/posts/my-post.html`; a
  `2026-08-01-` prefix is stripped, so date-prefixed files get clean URLs too.
- **`draft: true` keeps a post out of the build** until you're ready.

## Layout

```
posts/            your markdown — the only folder you edit day to day
blog.config.json  title, description, site URL, author
build.js          the generator (~250 lines, no dependencies)
server.js         local preview server
public/           the generated site — this is what you deploy
  style.css       the whole theme; the build never overwrites it
```

`public/` is a plain static site. Host it on GitHub Pages, Netlify, Cloudflare
Pages, S3, or anything that serves files. Set `url` in `blog.config.json` so the
RSS feed and canonical links point at your real domain.

## Making it yours

The theme is one CSS file — start with the variables at the top of
`public/style.css` for colors and measure. Page structure lives in the `layout`,
`renderIndex`, and `renderPost` functions in `build.js`.
